body:
Closes #170881

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes: an agent cannot reload a plugin used by its active turn because reload waits for that same turn to release the plugin.

## User Impact

Agents can call `plugins` with `action: "reloadAndResume"` and `pluginId` to end the turn, reload after normal cleanup, and continue the same conversation with the result. Existing direct reloads are unchanged; a CLI reload waiting inside its owning turn is not intercepted.

The handoff is process-local. After a Gateway exit before delivery, inspect plugin state before retrying. Stop cancels pending work but cannot undo a committed reload.

## Why This Change Was Made

Reuse the existing turn-yield callback, reply-owner settlement, authorized reload request, and same-session event delivery. No force-release, new RPC, database schema, CLI provenance detection, or replacement reload lifecycle. Caller permissions and session identity remain checked by their existing owners.

## Evidence

- 39 focused tests passed across the plugin tool, turn-abort wrapper, handoff, and real plugin-loader fixtures. The handoff test verifies yield → consumer cleanup → reload → one same-session result; failures, insufficient permissions, and Stop are covered.
- The prepared-runtime fixture releases a real generation-1 lease before loading generation 2. The initiating action survives the deliberate turn-yield abort; ordinary sibling calls still cancel.
- `pnpm build:runtime`, core type checking, the three touched test type graphs, typed lint, formatting, line-cap ratchet, and docs MDX checks passed. Fresh independent review found no actionable P0/P1 issues.
- No operator Gateway was changed. The additional runtime proof below uses a disposable real Gateway and authenticated WebSocket transport; inference is a deterministic loopback provider, not a live external model.

### Real session reproduction — unchanged head ce199636

[Download the redacted actual session trace (JSON)](https://91b59577e757131d68d55a471fe32aca.r2.cloudflarestorage.com/openclaw-crabbox-artifacts/crabbox-artifacts/v2/org/b3BlbmNsYXc/owner/Z2l0aHViOjU4NDkz/publish/delivery/20261013-212633-868278158/session-trace.json?X-Amz-Expires=604800&X-Amz-Date=20261013T212635Z&X-Amz-Algorithm=AWS4-HMAC-SHA256&X-Amz-Credential=a677a1e59413a4e54bcc2da5fec6164a%2F20261013%2Fauto%2Fs3%2Faws4_request&X-Amz-SignedHeaders=host&X-Amz-Signature=dd4f31ea5f23c400c71fe8a47b021c7d04cd6868660f445e9e0d02ed88ed04d2) — verified downloadable; expires **2026-10-20 21:26 UTC**. The full bounded trace is also embedded below so the proof remains readable after link expiry.

The normal candidate build completed an authenticated conversation in **16.251 seconds** (1/1 test passed): model calls reload_probe in fixture generation 1 → plugins.reloadAndResume → original turn ends → actual plugin disposal waits at a controlled barrier → release → the same conversation receives ok:true, calls the probe in fixture generation 2 with disposed:[1], and returns RELOAD_RESUME_DONE. Exactly one initial and one resumed effect; original input is not replayed. The resumed turn has a new run ID, not a new conversation. Fixture generation numbers are distinct from the Gateway registry generation in the reload receipt.

This positive trace came from the normal build before adding the scratch settlement observer. Node **24.19.0**, frozen dependency installation, isolated state/HOME/workspace, real built Gateway and signed/authenticated WebSocket connection; no injected Gateway dispatcher or force-released lease. Inference and the probe plugin are synthetic. This is not live-provider, browser Stop-button, or automatic CLI-interception proof.

### Additional authenticated cases

The expanded scratch matrix passed **9/9** at 21:08 UTC (127.48 seconds reported by Vitest). It used the same candidate plus a disclosed observer-only patch: after the detached handoff's existing final release, POST to a loopback settlement observer. That patch does not select, authorize, reload, or resume work and is **not in the PR**.

| Case | Observed result |
| --- | --- |
| Admin | Successful reload; same conversation; one initial and one resumed effect. |
| Read/write-only nonadmin | plugins is excluded: actual tool result Tool plugins not found; no reload or resumed effect. This is tool-exposure denial, not a reload-RPC denial. |
| Stop / device-token revoke during disposal | Handoff settles; no candidate registration or resumed effect. Stop acknowledges aborted:true. |
| Session reset | No old continuation effect. Recovery can publish a new registry; a separate diagnostic conversation invokes the **original** plugin revision. Not a claim of unchanged registry generation. |
| Delete and replace destination | No old continuation effect; replacement history stays empty. |
| Reassign responsibility | Reload and continuation succeed in the same session while preserving reassignment. Responsibility reassignment is not authorization revocation. |
| Stop / revoke after reload, before resumed tool effect | Reload result is already successful; candidate remains installed, but no resumed effect. Revoke reports loss of admitted/Gateway authority. |

For negative cases, the general claim is **settled with no resumed effect under the tested invalidation**; a final observer alone does not classify every possible error. Independent read-only evidence review confirmed these boundaries. Later mechanical harness lint/type cleanup passed typed lint and formatting; its E2E type graph passed, while the aggregate root graph exposed missing extension declarations removed by the scratch build. A subsequent replay failed at fixture HTTP readiness (before model execution), and the next build was interrupted by a host Gateway restart. Neither is counted as a product pass or as a regression fix.

The original external-turn comparator also passed its two boundary cases on base 1f972913787c5e2b7fa999c04a5fa5736a7f4d47: ordinary reload and --wait remain pending while the real prepared lease is open, then finish after normal lease cleanup. This comparator is an RPC/lease boundary reproduction, not a full model-launched CLI process reproduction.

The original CI run was red. The isolated server.sessions-send.test.ts replay passed all 10 tests on both the actual CI base and merge 6b15188cf691597066bae08acea928ab9e6e1407; this does **not** identify or fix the original 10-second timeout. No CI rerun, commit, push, merge, or deployment was made for that trace-publication update.

### Landing CI corrections — head 0bb311195e9a

The plugin-handoff production code is unchanged from trace-tested ce199636. The only net follow-up is preparing the real sessions-create handler beside the fixture's existing chat-handler preparation, before RPC deadlines start. A pinned-baseline diagnostic measured **6339 ms** for cold sessions-create import. The corrected full original shard passed **77 tests**, including the affected reply case at **1961 ms**. No timeout, assertions, authorization, storage, or production handler was changed. The original CI timeout was not reproduced; this removes measured cold-handler setup from the request budget, without claiming every possible timeout has been diagnosed.

The original wrapper failure is the existing upstream c9907c3 inventory correction, already present on main. Initially carrying it on this older branch passed all **20 local wrapper tests**, but CI38545800363 exposed a merge-only duplicate of chat-display-projection.helpers.ts because main moved the entry. The redundant carry-forward is removed at 0bb311195e9a. Reconstructing the merge against the tested base38ace3094c390026fc384615c005693dc17c918f now yields exactly one entry and an inventory blob **identical to main** (afcd7aad2ed08d8e4033bf07a8caf3fb8e7b0652); all **20 wrapper owner tests pass** on that main inventory. This leaves main as the sole inventory owner, with no net wrapper change in this PR.

The selected Gateway-server type graph, typed lint, formatting, and diff checks passed. Fresh independent Codex reviews found no actionable P0/P1 issue in the original candidate or the CI correction delta. The reviewed fixture preparation is unchanged by the inventory carry-forward removal. Current-head PR CI is the required broad gate; earlier reds and passing local replays are not labeled green CI. The original behavior trace remains valid for the unchanged handoff implementation.

