## Work sessions

- [Original discussion with @jacobtomlinson](https://team.openclaw.ai/chat/roboclaw/dashboard/9fd5643e-4726-470d-89bc-c58d58cef773)
- [Implementation and verification](https://team.openclaw.ai/chat/roboclaw/dashboard/1692a10c-f035-48d4-b6ef-a8210878de45)

## What Problem This Solves

Session-only readers should refresh the specific Actions query that was approved. The original PR already limits delegation to `github.actions.runs` and explicitly granted repositories, but readers can manually vary supported queries within those repositories using the agent's credentials.

## User Impact

Saved summaries should use the existing `session:report` mechanism. Live widgets declare named queries fixing repository, workflow, branch, status/date filters, result limit, pull-request handling, and response fields. Session-only readers send exactly `{ ticket, queryName }`; the Gateway resolves the stored definition and rejects selectors, overrides, and flexible fallback calls. Repository-only grants do not implicitly authorize named queries.

Approval deliberately shares selected private and future matching results obtained with the agent's GitHub credentials. Every semantic definition edit, including narrowing, renews approval under the existing Full/Auto/Ask policy. Existing repository-only HTML widgets need an approved named query for session-only live refresh; they are not converted automatically. General Gateway readers retain their existing flexible API and repository-grant contract.

## Implementation

This uses the existing widget declaration, grant store, approval policy, RPC, Actions adapter, and credential-scoped transport cache. It adds no permission store, table, config option, protocol version, or RPC. The protected bridge and authoring tool use `openclaw.data.query(name)`.

The Gateway preserves current session visibility, widget revision/generation, exact grant, credential, and revocation checks through final delivery, including cached/shared results. Pending and granted approval details display the complete query and its private/future-result sharing consequence, while retaining localized network/tool labels, bold group headings, and bullet lists.

This is a product/authorization-scope repair, not an assertion that the original PR necessarily violates `SECURITY.md`.

## Evidence

Published signed head: `18ff63ac963d898a22ee5909e5ae161d459c601c`. Current-head [CI run 41926013692](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/41926013692): **completed successfully: 86 jobs passed, 19 intentionally skipped, no failures**; the required `openclaw/ci-gate` is **successful**.

- **Authorization:** final focused validation passed **12 files / 246 tests** across unit-fast, Gateway, and UI. Expected-red proof on original head `dd12924919e2bb7179bdbf0b19d4de1121ba7bba` showed a manually widened flexible Actions call returning metadata through a repository-only grant. Repaired tests cover exact named-query admission, manual overrides/fallback, legacy grants, definition-edit renewal, field projection, credential/cache isolation, and revocation through final delivery. Gateway/browser generation checks, scoped formatting, locale verification, bridge and merged-schema consumers pass.
- **Real private GitHub and authenticated Gateway WebSockets:** distinct profiles; reader scopes exactly `operator.sessions.read`; anonymous repository access returned 404. Expanded proof passed in 46.966s (626.05s command including preparation): 67 captured records, 30 response envelopes, 9 denials without payload, and 2 native Actions HTTP 200 responses. Warm reads made no additional Actions request. Holding a native Response after real HTTP receipt, revoking visibility by RPC, then releasing it delivered no reader payload; owner positive controls succeeded. Selected actual records are below.
- **Stable upgrade/restart:** `v2026.10.10` (`7dc3350cb9f08fcf9a0af5c4845a20d5b2f1a8be`, still the latest stable release at verification) wrote three HTML widgets and ordinary/repository-only grants. Normal `openclaw doctor --fix --non-interactive` migrated agent schema 27→40 and shared schema 22→39 and exited 0. Complete snapshots and legacy documents compared exactly; legacy grants gained no queries. A new query required explicit approval, and its complete approved snapshot survived a separate process. Executed candidate `486a0fd`; all 20 production board/schema files remain equivalent through the merged head. Actual output is below.
- **UI:** the current built-English browser check passes (27.10s), confirming bold headings, disc bullets, complete query/field/sharing disclosure, keyboard interaction, grant-to-pending transition, and no horizontal overflow. The locale regression reproduced red and was repaired; French built-browser pending/granted checks passed (40.92s before / 40.75s after). Inspected synthetic before/after screenshots are embedded below.
- **Performance:** current-head CI passes the existing base-aware checker. Chat is **1,285,849 B**, **213 B below** the owner-approved **1,286,062 B** cap (base 1,285,137 B, +712 B). Startup is 92,696→92,735 B (+39 B); New is 799,440→799,569 B (+129 B). Requests and CSS are unchanged. The accepted Chat cap retains main's existing 192 B compression/name variance allowance; the shared budget policy and restored main startup baseline are unchanged.
- **Review:** the final hosted [review](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/186057#issuecomment-6446007151) inspected exact product head `00d890768f270c031b10cd504700a143f1e4f1df` and reported no findings. The authorization/query owners and product UI are unchanged from that reviewed, live-tested implementation. CI follow-through received independent source review plus API-backed local Codex Auto Review at P0–P2: the seven-file CI delta against `00d8907` and the two-file authority-fixture delta against `b9fa1cb` each completed one pass with `scoped-clean` and final snapshot verification. The latter empty sandbox lacked surrounding source/test execution; the separate source review and actual VM/CI runs provide that evidence. Subsequent changes only rename an unused disposable binding and shrink the allocation, with targeted proof.

### CI follow-through and regression cost

Minimal fixture repairs use canonical English/German UI labels without weakening assertions. The disposable Android emulator uses a 4 GiB userdata partition; trust cleanup waits for ADB readiness inside the existing 60-second deadline. Namespace restoration, certificate-baseline, SELinux, uninstall and unconditional disposal checks remain. The cleanup regression is red on the original script and passes 28 checks with the repair (27.10s Vitest, 35.49s command). Both current-head native jobs pass. Independently inspected prior-head artifacts each contain all 13 required native cases, verified APK hashes/ABIs and zipalign, restoration of all 149 baseline certificates, mount removal, SELinux Enforcing, absent task packages, emulator termination and AVD deletion, with no cleanup-failure artifact.

The inherited Workshop authority fixture revoked its receipt but returned true once, with `setImmediate` choosing an unreliable boundary. It now revokes after the actual skill-directory `mkdir` resolves and always reports live authority. The real loaded plugin/tool/Gateway path, authorized control, exact refusal and actual file-absence assertion remain. A guest-only negative control removed caller-authority forwarding while retaining the outer wrapper: the wrapper refused, but the file was written, and exactly the absence assertion failed (83.47s). The production file was restored byte-for-byte. Restored authority 2/2 and skill-authoring owner 8/8 pass (64.97s / 72.87s Vitest; 96.33s / 91.14s command). The agents-root typecheck passes (248.60s); targeted lint and formatting pass. Exact current-head CI also executes both authority cases successfully, with the revoked trace reporting `SKILL.md exists: false`.

Simplification retains the existing owners and removes the timer/one-permissive-check fixture behavior. Against integrated main `079d8d92`, handwritten source is +261/−41 (net +220), tests +1015/−64 (net +951), generated validators +605/−414 (net +191), and docs +94. Necessary growth implements the closed query contract, exact approval comparison, server resolution, final projection and complete disclosure. Current main already contains the three-line German fixture update, so the canonical current-base test delta and task-specific allocation are **948**. The shared scalar is unchanged. Current-head ratchets pass: 6,474,662 test LF bytes, ceiling 6,474,686, slack 24.

<details>
<summary>Local browser failure retained as an unexplained setup observation</summary>

The full two browser files initially passed 20/21 cases (404.67s), including all five repaired browser failures. The untouched cron sibling, “keeps selected task history when a deferred overview refresh resolves last”, failed initial setup before its test body (`routeReady=false`, `rosterReady=true`, no browser errors). One bounded isolated replay passed (75.11s); the original failure remains unexplained and is not claimed fixed or pre-existing. Current-head selected UI CI lanes pass. The full chat-controls file passes 15/15 (81.71s).

</details>

Live proof uses real HTTP/WebSockets with existing config/runtime/plugin-registry and unrelated startup harness seams; it does not establish deployed-Gateway, model-inference, packaged-updater or native-app behavior. No merge or deployment is requested.

